      "flux": "#!/bin/bash\n\n# Flux directives translated from LSF\n# The original script requests 600 nodes and launches 3600 total tasks (6 tasks/node).\n#FLUX: -N 600\n#FLUX: -n 3600\n#FLUX: -t 0:10:00\n#FLUX: -o color-3600g.o%J\n\n# The LSF directive '#BSUB -alloc_flags gpumps' and jsrun '-g1' flag imply a\n# request for one GPU per task. This is translated to a total GPU count.\n#FLUX: -g 3600\n\n#\n# --- Omitted LSF options and explanations ---\n#\n# -P CSC275MCCLURE\n#   Omitted. Flux accounting is typically managed through system configuration or job\n#   submission options, not a standard batch directive.\n#\n# -J COLOR\n#   Omitted. The job name in Flux is generally set during submission (e.g.,\n#   'flux submit --job-name=COLOR ...') or via the FLUX_JOB_NAME\n#   environment variable, not through a script directive.\n#\n# -env \"all,JOB_FEATURE=NVME\"\n#   Omitted. This directive was commented out in the original script. Requesting\n#   node features like NVME in Flux is typically handled at submission time with a\n#   constraint, e.g., 'flux submit --requires=nvme'.\n#\n# jsrun -r6 -c1 -brs --smpiargs=\"-gpu\"\n#   Omitted. These are highly specific IBM jsrun options for resource and process\n#   layout (6 resource sets per node, 1 core per resource set, etc.). Flux uses\n#   its own mechanisms for process placement and binding.\n#   Performance implication: This is a critical omission. Without the detailed binding\n#   and layout hints, the default task layout provided by Flux may result in sub-optimal\n#   NUMA locality and communication patterns, potentially degrading performance for this\n#   large-scale, tightly-coupled MPI application.\n#\n\ndate\n\nmodule load gcc cuda \n#source $OLCF_SPECTRUM_MPI_ROOT/jsm_pmix/bin/export_smpi_env -gpu\n\n#cd /ccs/home/mcclurej/summit/build/ScaLBL/example/Sph1896/\n\n#cp Domain.in.8g Domain.in\n\nexport LBPM_WIA_DIR=$HOME/summit/build/LBPM-WIA/tests\n\ncd /gpfs/alpinetds/csc275/scratch/mcclurej/SCALING/WEAK/3600p\n\n# 'jsrun' is replaced by 'flux run'. 'flux run' will automatically launch the 3600\n# tasks requested by the '#FLUX: -n 3600' directive across the 600 nodes.\nflux run  $LBPM_WIA_DIR/TestCommD3Q19\n\n\nexit;\n",
