              "content": "#!/bin/bash\n\n# Flux directives translated from SLURM\n#FLUX: -q GPU\n#FLUX: -g 1\n#FLUX: -n 10\n#FLUX: -c 1\n#FLUX: -t 01:00:00\n\n#\n# --- Omitted SLURM options and explanations ---\n#\n# --job-name=gromacstest\n#   Omitted. The job name in Flux is generally set during submission (e.g.,\n#   'flux submit --job-name=gromacstest ...') or via the FLUX_JOB_NAME\n#   environment variable, not through a script directive.\n#\n# Note: The original SLURM script did not request a specific amount of memory.\n# This job will receive the default memory allocation from the scheduler.\n#\n\nexport GMX_IMGDIR=${SIFDIR}/gromacs/\nexport GMX_IMG=gromacs-2022.3_20230206.sif\nexport TOPOL_FILE=topol.tpr\n\n# CRITICAL: The original command is a mix of MPI and OpenMP parallelism designed\n# for a single MPI task and multiple OpenMP threads. However, the SLURM directives\n# requested 10 MPI tasks. This is a fundamental contradiction.\n# This translation preserves the request for 10 tasks in the Flux directives.\n# The GROMACS command is being run as 10 separate, independent Singularity\n# containers via 'flux run'. Each will attempt to use OpenMP threads, leading to\n# oversubscription and poor performance. The original command's intent is unclear,\n# but running a 10-task MPI job would typically look like:\n# 'flux run -n 10 singularity ... gmx_mpi mdrun -nb gpu ...'\n\n# The SLURM environment variable ${SLURM_NTASKS} is replaced by ${FLUX_JOB_NTASKS}.\nflux run singularity run --nv -B ${PWD}:/host_pwd --pwd /host_pwd $GMX_IMGDIR/$GMX_IMG gmx mdrun -ntmpi 1 -nb gpu -pin on -v -noconfout -nsteps 5000 -ntomp ${FLUX_JOB_NTASKS} -s $TOPOL_FILE\n#DO NOT USE 'srun' as it launches multiple independent jobs\n"
