              "content": "#!/bin/bash\n# Flux job script translated from an LSF script generator.\n\n# The LSF project/account directive was not translated as it is not a standard Flux option.\n# This is typically handled by site-specific scheduler policies.\n# #BSUB -P MED106\n\n#flux: --job-name=ytopt\n#flux: -t 00:30:00\n\n# The job resource requirements are derived from the BSUB directives and the jsrun command.\n# This requests 4096 nodes and a total of 4096 tasks, which Flux will distribute as 1 per node.\n# Each task is allocated 42 CPUs.\n#flux: -N 4096\n#flux: -n 4096\n#flux: -c 42\n\n# CRITICAL: The LSF allocation flag 'gpumps' has no direct equivalent in Flux and was OMITTED.\n# This flag enables GPU Multi-Process Service, and its absence may lead to incorrect behavior\n# or significantly degraded performance if the application relies on it.\n# #BSUB -alloc_flags gpumps\n\n# The original script specified separate output and error files. Flux merges these into a single file by default with the -o flag.\n#flux: -o output.log\n\n# NOTE: OMP_NUM_THREADS is set to 168, while only 42 CPUs are requested per task.\n# This implies a 4x oversubscription of threads to cores, likely to leverage SMT (hyperthreading).\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=168\n\n# 'flux run' replaces 'jsrun' and inherits the job allocation.\n# The original script ran 5 identical commands serially.\nflux run ../amg2013 -laplace -P 16 16 16 -n 100 100 100 > out.txt\nflux run ../amg2013 -laplace -P 16 16 16 -n 100 100 100 > out2.txt\nflux run ../amg2013 -laplace -P 16 16 16 -n 100 100 100 > out3.txt\nflux run ../amg2013 -laplace -P 16 16 16 -n 100 100 100 > out4.txt\nflux run ../amg2013 -laplace -P 16 16 16 -n 100 100 100 > out5.txt\n"
