      "slurm": "#!/bin/bash\n\n# Flux batch script converted from PBS\n# Note: Flux does not support in-script directives for resource specification\n# This script should be submitted with the following command:\n# flux batch -N 1 -n 1 -c 1 -g 1 -t 24h --setattr=system.memory=30720M --job-name=QCL_PT script.sh\n#\n# Resource specifications:\n# -N 1         : 1 node (from select=1)\n# -n 1         : 1 task (from ncpus=1, interpreted as 1 task)\n# -c 1         : 1 CPU core per task (from ncpus=1)\n# -g 1         : 1 GPU (from ngpus=1)\n# -t 24h       : 24 hour walltime (from walltime=24:00:00)\n# --setattr=system.memory=30720M : 30GB memory (from mem=30GB, 30 * 1024 MB)\n# --job-name=QCL_PT : job name/project (from -P QCL_PT)\n#\n# PBS resource calculation:\n# select=1:ncpus=1:ngpus=1:mem=30GB means 1 chunk with 1 CPU, 1 GPU, 30GB memory\n# Flux equivalent: -N 1 -n 1 -c 1 -g 1 with total memory 30GB\n\n# Original project/account: QCL_PT\n# Note: PBS -P directive typically maps to account/project allocation\n# Flux uses banks/accounts through flux-accounting\n# Set at submission if flux-accounting is configured:\n# flux batch --setattr=bank=QCL_PT ...\n# Performance impact: Without bank specification, job may run under default account\n# which could affect priority, resource limits, or accounting tracking\n\n# Original queue: defaultQ\n# Note: Flux does not have queues/partitions in the same way as PBS\n# Queue selection is handled through Flux's scheduling configuration\n# Performance impact: Job scheduling depends on site-specific Flux queue policies\n# Consult site documentation for equivalent resource pools or queues\n\n# GPU allocation: 1 GPU\n# Note: PBS specifies ngpus=1 (generic GPU request)\n# Flux uses -g 1 for GPU count allocation\n# GPU type/model cannot be specified in standard Flux syntax\n# Performance impact: Job may be allocated any available GPU type\n# Consult site-specific Flux configuration for GPU property constraints\n\n# Memory: 30GB\n# Note: PBS specifies mem=30GB for total memory in the chunk\n# Flux memory specification: --setattr=system.memory=30720M (MB)\n# Alternative syntax may be --setattr=memory=30720M depending on Flux version\n# Performance impact: Without explicit memory request, job gets default allocation\n# which may be insufficient and cause OOM errors\n\n# Module loads\nmodule load cuda/10.0.130\nmodule load openmpi-gcc/3.1.3\n\n# Activate conda environment\nsource miniconda/bin/activate training\n\n# Change to working directory\ncd models/\n\n# Run TensorFlow 2 object detection training\n# Direct execution without MPI launcher since this is a single-task GPU job\npython research/object_detection/model_main_tf2.py \\\n  --model_dir=\"/project/RDS-FSC-QCL_PT-RW/rcnn_model_data/\" \\\n  --num_train_steps=200000 \\\n  --sample_1_of_n_eval_examples=1 \\\n  --pipeline_config_path=pipeline.config \\\n  --alsologtostderr\n\n# Note: Fixed typo in original script: '--alsologtostder' -> '--alsologtostderr'\n# Note: The original uses single-CPU, single-GPU configuration\n# TensorFlow will utilize the GPU through CUDA\n# Ensure CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES is properly set by Flux for GPU allocation\n\n```\n\n### REQUIREMENTS & CONSTRAINTS\nYou MUST return a JSON structure with fields for 'valid' (bool) and a list of string 'reasons'. You MAY optionally add a field `issues` with a list of debugging or critique of any code.\n\n### INSTRUCTIONS\n1. Analyze the provided script above.\n2. Use MUST use a jobspec validation tool for the manager if one is available otherwise use your knowledge.\n3. Determine validity based on the resource section ONLY\n4. The 'reasons' should ONLY be specific to resources\n5. If the script includes problematic code, please include in 'issues'\n6. You MUST include a 'tool_call' boolean, true or false, to indicate if you used a validation tool for validation.\n7. Return a json structure with 'valid' and 'tool_call' and 'reasons' (if not valid) and 'issues'",
